## Layout

```
[[ course_code | replace(' ', '') ]]/
├── README.md               # human-facing guide to the machinery below
├── AGENTS.md / CLAUDE.md   # this file (CLAUDE.md just imports it)
├── Makefile                # every build + scaffolding command (see below)
├── tex/
│   ├── course.sty          # shared preamble: course/student info, macros, envs, styles
│   ├── hw_template.tex     # homework template (used by `make new-hw`)
│   └── lecture_template.tex  # lecture-notes template (used by `make new-lec`)
├── scripts/
│   ├── course.sh           # scaffolding helpers behind the Makefile
│   └── course.conf         # course-specific names (slide prefix, official PDF pattern)
├── course/                 # instructor material not tied to one assignment — read-only
│   ├── slides/             # lecNN-<topic>.pdf, lecNN-<topic>-annotated.pdf ("[[ annotated_marker ]]" versions)
│   └── readings/           # papers, textbook chapters
├── notes/                  # the user's own lecture notes
│   └── NN-<topic>/
│       ├── lecNN.tex
│       ├── figures/
│       └── build/          # latexmk output: lecNN.pdf, synctex, aux files
├── homework/
│   └── hwN/
│       ├── hwN.tex         # the user's solutions (this is the file to edit)
│       ├── build/          # latexmk output: hwN.pdf (what gets submitted), synctex, aux files
│       ├── figures/        # images referenced by hwN.tex
│       ├── code/           # the user's working copy of starter code + data it needs
│       └── handout/        # course-provided files — read-only, never edit
│           ├── [[ hw_pdf_pattern | replace('{N}', 'N') ]]   # official assignment PDF (source of truth)
│           ├── main.tex                    # problem source
│           └── ...                         # original figures, starter code, data
├── exams/                  # practice exams + the user's review sheets
└── projects/               # course projects, one folder each
```

Rule of thumb: instructor material is read-only and lives either in `course/` or, when it
belongs to one assignment, in that assignment's `handout/`; everything else is the user's.
Keep official homework PDFs named `[[ hw_pdf_pattern | replace('{N}', 'N') ]]` — never `hwN.pdf`, which is the
build output's name. Starter code expects its data file beside it, so copy data into `code/`
too; `handout/` keeps the untouched originals for diffing.

Every `.tex` document lives exactly two levels below the root and loads the shared preamble via
`\makeatletter\def\input@path{{../../tex/}}\makeatother \usepackage{course}` — keep new
documents at that depth.

## Commands (run from the repo root)

- **Build:** `make hw2`, `make lec03`, `make homework`, `make notes`, or `make` for everything.
  All output (PDF, synctex, aux) goes to the document's `build/`. `make watch-hw2` rebuilds on
  save; `make clean-hw2` / `make clean` drop aux files but keep PDFs; `make distclean` removes
  every `build/`. These wrap `latexmk -pdf -outdir=build <doc>.tex` — if calling latexmk directly,
  always pass `-outdir=build`. Always compile after editing and fix errors/overfull boxes.
- **New homework:** `make new-hw N=3 [DUE="Mon Oct 19, 11:59 PM PT"]` creates `homework/hw3/`
  with `hw3.tex` (fields filled), `figures/`, `code/`, `handout/`.
- **New lecture notes:** `make new-lec TOPIC="Junction Trees" [N=6] [DATE="..."]` creates
  `notes/06-junction-trees/lec06.tex` (N defaults to next; DATE to today, `DATE=""` for none).
- **Slides:** drop `[[ slide_prefix ]] NN.pdf` (and the annotated version, whose name contains
  `[[ annotated_marker ]]`) into `course/slides/`, then
  `make slides` (also run by `new-lec`) renames them to `lecNN-<topic>[-annotated].pdf` using the
  topic of `notes/NN-<topic>/`.
- **VS Code:** `.vscode/` has LaTeX Workshop settings (build on save to `build/`, no auto-clean)
  and tasks `LaTeX: Compile`, `LaTeX: Clean`, `LaTeX: Compile + Clean` (Cmd+Shift+B).

## Homework workflow (LaTeX)

- **New assignment:** `make new-hw N=…`, put the course's files in `handout/`, then transcribe
  each problem from `handout/main.tex`/PDF into a `problem` box followed by a `solution` block.
  Copy figures into `figures/` with clean filenames (no spaces/parens), or redraw simple
  diagrams in TikZ with the `bnnode`/`bnedge` styles so they blend into the problem box.
- Transcribe problem statements **verbatim** — don't paraphrase, reorder, or "fix" them.
  Cross-check against the official PDF, which is the source of truth if it differs from `main.tex`.
- Each problem ends with `\clearpage` so Gradescope page-matching is one problem per page.
- Submission is `homework/hwN/build/hwN.pdf` via Gradescope.

## Lecture notes

- The user writes their own notes in `notes/NN-<topic>/lecNN.tex`; the instructor's slides are
  in `course/slides/` (cite them in notes with `\slide{12}`).
- Note-taking environments (from `course.sty`): `definition`, `theorem`, `lemma`, `proposition`,
  `corollary`, `claim`, `example`, `remark`, plus boxes `keyidea` (takeaways), `question`
  (follow-ups), `aside` (rigor/connections beyond lecture). Numbered per section in notes.

## Shared LaTeX conventions (`tex/course.sty` — use these, don't reinvent)

- Homework environments: `\begin{problem}{1.1}{Title}{pts}`, `\begin{solution}`, `\part{a}` for
  sub-part headings inside a solution (`\part*{...}` drops the parentheses); in homework,
  `enumerate` defaults to (a), (b), ...
- Macros: `\P` (probability, plain P), `\E`, `\Var`, `\Cov`, `\KL{p}{q}`, `\given` (conditioning bar),
  `\indep` / `\nindep` (conditional independence ⫫), `\ind{...}` (indicator), `\pa` (parents),
  `\iid`, `\argmax`, `\argmin`, `\R`, `\N`, `\Normal`.
- Belief networks: TikZ with the `bnnode` / `bnedge` styles (filled with `probbg`, the problem-box
  background); the `bayesnet` library (`\node[latent]`, `\node[obs]`, `\edge`) is also loaded.
- Code in write-ups: `lstlisting` (Python is the default language).
- Course/student info (`\coursename`, `\instructor`, `\studentname`, ...) is set once in `course.sty`.
- If a new macro is broadly useful, add it to `course.sty` **and** mention it here.

[% if use_python %]
## Code projects (Python)

- Python via uv: `pyproject.toml`/`uv.lock` at the root, environment in the in-repo `.venv/`
  (gitignored; no direnv). Run things with `uv run …`; add packages with `uv add`. Prefer
  numpy/scipy; matplotlib for plots. Code goes in `homework/hwN/code/` or `projects/<name>/`.
- Keep scripts reproducible: fix random seeds, save figures to a `figures/` folder as PDF
  (vector) so they drop straight into the LaTeX write-up.
- Be careful with numerical stability (work in log-space for likelihoods/products of
  probabilities, use `logsumexp`).

[% endif %]
## How to help

- Default to being a rigorous collaborator: checking proofs for gaps, catching edge cases
  (zero-probability conditioning events, support assumptions, discrete vs. continuous),
  explaining concepts, debugging LaTeX and code.
- **Do not write full homework solutions into `hwN.tex` unless the user explicitly asks.**
  Course academic-integrity rules apply; prefer hints, verification of the user's own
  work, and LaTeX/typesetting help.
- Never modify anything in `handout/` or `course/` (course-provided `main.tex`, official PDFs,
  slides, original starter code) — renaming new drop-ins via `make slides` is the exception.
- The repo is under git. Keep any remote **private**: it holds homework solutions and the
  instructor's materials.
